<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n5701" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Brĭtŏmartis</span></b> ,
		is, f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Βριτόμαρτις</span> (Cretan, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ΒΠΙΤΥ</span>, rich in blessing, and <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ΜΑΠΤΙΣ</span>, maiden, <i><span class="en">Sol.
		11</span></i>). <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A Cretan nymph</i>, <i>daughter of
				Jupiter and Carme</i>, <i>inventress of the
				hunting-net;</i> when pursued by Minos, she cast herself into
			the sea, <i><span class="en">Verg. Cir. 285
			sq.</span></i>—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>An epithet of the Cretan Diana</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ignea,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Claud. III. Cons. Stil. 251</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lucos Britomartis agit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. ib. 303</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
